From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 22:47:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fix stylesheet fetch typing, decoding and stale deliveries
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

load_linked_stylesheet stamps `content_type = "text/css"` so the
embedder's NetProvider can tell a stylesheet load from a generic one
without peeking at the NetHandler type. StylesheetLoader's nested
@import fetch never got the same stamp, so those requests fell through
to URL-extension inference. Any @import whose URL carries no `.css`
extension resolved to a generic resource kind and lost the
stylesheet-specific handling the top-level sheet receives.
Wikipedia-style `load.php?modules=...` imports are the common shape.

Whether a stylesheet's bytes may ever be handed to page JS depends on
its fetch mode, and only the DOM knows that: a `<link>` with a
`crossorigin` attribute fetches in mode "cors", and without one it
fetches "no-cors", whose response is opaque even when the server sends
Access-Control-Allow-Origin. Rather than add a field to blitz-traits'
Request, the mode rides on the same `content_type` tag as a MIME
parameter: `text/css;ks-cors=cors`. StylesheetLoader carries the mode so
an @import chain inherits what its root sheet declared, per CSSOM. An
inline `<style>` has no crossorigin attribute, so its imports are
"no-cors".

NetHandler::bytes bailed with Err("Invalid UTF8") on any sheet that
wasn't valid UTF-8, silently discarding it. sigarra.up.pt serves its
2022 theme and base sheet as iso-8859-15, so the page lost its masthead
gradient and every screen-only visibility rule while pure-ASCII sheets
kept applying; a half-styled render that looked like a layout bug.
Follow css-syntax §3.2: BOM, then @charset, then strict UTF-8, with a
windows-1252 fallback for legacy bytes, via the already-vendored
encoding_rs. Applies to @import'ed sheets too.

load_resource handed a Resource::Css to add_stylesheet_for_node without
checking that the <link> still exists. Script can remove the link while
its sheet is still in flight. When the node was dropped, its slotmap key
went stale and add_stylesheet_for_node panicked with "invalid SlotMap
key used". When the node was only detached, its sheet styled a document
the link was no longer part of. Nothing removes that sheet, because the
removal ran before the sheet was registered.

The load is now dropped unless the node is a live element in the
document, as Chrome does. Inserting the link again starts a new fetch
through process_added_subtree. A Resource::Css with no node id is
dropped instead of unwrapped.

Co-authored-by: Luís Duarte <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
